<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for the macOS app, in both identities
    `mac/scripts/bundle-iced.sh` assembles: Roost.app (production) and
    Roost-Iced.app (dev builds). bundle-iced.sh signs the main executable
    with this file and the `runtime` option. tools/roosttest_unit/
    test_mac_entitlements.py pins the keys below, and CI's "Assert bundle
    contents" step checks the signed bundle carries them.

    Editing note: no double hyphens anywhere in this comment. AMFI's XML
    parser rejects the sequence even inside a comment.

    com.apple.security.device.audio-input
    com.apple.security.device.camera
    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events

    Roost is the TCC *responsible app* for the programs it hosts: when a
    child process in a tab (Claude Code's /voice, ffmpeg, sox, an
    osascript automation) touches the microphone, camera, or sends Apple
    events, macOS attributes the request to Roost, not the child. Under
    the hardened runtime that attribution is *silently denied* unless
    Roost carries the matching resource entitlement: no prompt, no
    Privacy pane entry, no error. These three let those requests prompt
    ("A program running in Roost would like to use the microphone")
    instead of failing silently. The paired purpose strings live in
    Info.plist.template. cf. ghostty and cmux, which carry the same
    capture set.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation

    bundle-iced.sh embeds an ad-hoc-signed Sparkle.framework, dlopen'd at
    runtime under the hardened runtime. Library validation insists every
    loaded library be signed by the SAME Team ID as the main executable
    (or be a platform binary). A dev build ships ad-hoc-signed (no Team
    ID), so without this entitlement the hardened runtime would refuse
    the framework: a load failure at updater-init time.

    Release Developer-ID builds (release.yml's `mac` job) are team-signed
    and STILL carry this entitlement. Once the app and Sparkle.framework
    are both signed by our Team ID, same-team signing satisfies library
    validation and the entitlement is no longer needed for them (cf.
    Ghostty, which is team-signed and carries no LV-disable); dropping it
    there is a separate entitlements hygiene pass, not yet done.

    What we INTENTIONALLY do not need:

    * `cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` / `cs.allow-jit`
      libghostty-vt is linked as a static archive (its code is part of
      Roost's own signed image), no JIT, no dynamically generated
      machine code at runtime.

    * `network.client` / `network.server` Roost does not speak over a
      network socket; the IPC server is UNIX-domain only. (Sparkle's
      update download runs in its own XPC service, which carries its
      own networking. Host sessions reach other machines through the
      user's own `ssh`, a child process.)

    * App Sandbox flags sandboxing the shells Roost spawns would defeat
      the product. cmux makes the same choice.

    * com.apple.security.personal-information.{addressbook,calendars,
      location,photos-library} ghostty carries these; we deliberately do
      NOT. A TCC grant to Roost is inherited by *every* child it ever
      spawns, so the entitlement set is the blast radius. Capture devices
      (mic/camera) light a menu-bar in-use indicator; PIM reads have
      none, so a buggy or hostile agent could read the whole address
      book invisibly. We add a PIM key only if a concrete Roost feature
      needs it, not speculatively.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
